    "md5": "33037a39a5c3a8891ba517f40a93a619",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1889,
    "chunk_text": "The next court date is in February 2026 perhaps that is why the Australian side is now redoubling its support to the Australian mining companies! But will failed public diplomacy result in slowing down the protests or will it spur them on? Journalists should be aware of the complexity of the issues in Mannar and reflect all sides instead of just reporting on seminars and press briefings by one plainly well-endowed party.\n\nIndia is also now a major manufacturer of pharmaceuticals and their efforts to promote exports are running into controversies in the region. In Sri Lanka protests are escalating on account of recent acceptance of Indian standards (pharmacopia) at a time when contaminated imported vaccines and injections have been found in Sri Lanka public hospitals. Less known and perhaps more of concern, is that India is also now a leading manufacturer of defence equipment and is pushing its products through bilateral defence agreements such as the Dornier aircraft, which package comes with the embedding of Indian security personnel ostensibly for \u201ctraining\u201d and \u201cmaintenance\u201d. How do we balance bilateral assistance and investment needs and still ensure that Sri Lanka maintains its sovereignty and independence?\n\nOne way the press can help is by exposing the underlying conditions of multilateral and bilateral agreements. In any analysis of the different flows of foreign assistance into Sri Lanka, there is a need to highlight the direct cash transfers which support the Treasury and are the most valuable for the government. Remittances received from expatriates fall into this category and the newspapers carry countless stories of our local heroes who have been in the forefront of raising assistance for Sri Lanka. A number of countries even small neighbours like Maldives, Nepal and Bhutan have also provided direct donations to the Sri Lanka Government."
