    "md5": "e4bd386538038bbe060b1442710942cb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1914,
    "chunk_text": "Research carried out in 2016 by Sri Lankan scientists, using Geographic Information System (GIS) techniques, highlights the gravity of the situation. By analysing more than 84,000 plant location records across 2,847 grid cells of 5 km \u00d7 5 km, researchers identified nine major endemic areas: the Central Highlands, South Western Wet Zone, Northern Highlands, Eastern Highlands, Ritigala, Dolu Kanda, Yala, Wilpattu and Jaffna.\n\nIt is these very regions that were most affected by Cyclone Ditwah.\n\nDitwah, which developed in the Bay of Bengal, brought intense rainfall, strong winds and widespread flooding across large parts of the island. In the Central Highlands, prolonged rain triggered landslides and severe soil erosion in montane forests such as Knuckles, Horton Plains and Adam\u2019s Peak. Scientists warn that landslides can strip entire slopes of vegetation, wiping out endemic plant populations confined to small altitudinal belts.\n\nIn the South Western Wet Zone, including areas around the Sinharaja World Heritage Site, flooding and prolonged soil saturation have stressed endemic understory plants, while wind damage to the forest canopy has altered microclimatic conditions crucial for regeneration.\n\nProf. Wijesundara also drew attention to a less visible but equally serious consequence:\n\n\u201cDisturbed habitats created by landslides and floods are highly vulnerable to invasive alien species. Once invasives establish, they can permanently alter forest structure and outcompete endemic plants, posing a long-term threat that extends well beyond the cyclone itself.\u201d\n\nExperts stress that integrating biodiversity data with disaster risk information is now essential. Overlaying maps of endemic flowering plant distributions with GIS-based data on rainfall intensity, flood extent and landslide susceptibility can help identify areas where biological richness coincides with high disaster exposure."
  },
