    "md5": "daa6d404a45802edbd32b5dcc37e207d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2031,
    "chunk_text": "Maxi Rozairo\u2026What a voice! What a legend!\n\nNoeline Honter, the renowned Sri Lankan singer, TV presenter and English trainer, who is now based in Australia, sent us the following, describing her memories with the late Maxi Rozairo, who passed away on 27th December, 2025, age 77.\n\nMaxi, with his smoky voice and distinctive soulful style, had a rich alluring quality that captivated audiences whenever and wherever he performed.\n\nI first met Maxi, in early 1970, when he was singing with one of Sri Lanka\u2019s (then Ceylon) super groups of the time, the Spitfires. I was with the original X-Periments\u2026both outfits managed by Marcy Perera, fondly called \u201cUncle Marcy\u201d by all of us.\n\nThe two groups performed at the \u2018in\u2019 place in Colombo, the ballroom in Ceylinco which was the only \u2018high rise\u2019 building in Ceylon at the time. This was the gathering place for Colombo\u2019s \u2018jetset\u2019 who would flock there in droves every weekend.\n\nNoeline Honter and Maxi: A decade ago, inColombo, after a rehearsal\n\nThe Spitfires left for Dubai, on a long-term contract, in 1972, managed by Daisy Perera (\u201cAunty Daisy\u201d), the wife of Marcy Perera, together with bandleader Chinthi Perera, the outstanding drummer of the group.\n\nI joined them early the next year, at the plush Wagon Train nightclub in Dubai, working closely with Maxi and another legendary singer, Priyanthi Manamperi (later Jalaldeen, following her marriage to the group\u2019s amazing guitarist Raja Jalaldeen).\n\nThis stellar group, The Spitfires, composed of some of Sri Lanka\u2019s undeniably excellent, leading musicians, toured the Middle East for seven consecutive years, captivating audiences in Beirut, Damascus, Tehran and Isfahan in Iran, Cairo Egypt, Baghdad Iraq, and returning for repeat performances at a number of these venues.\n\nMaxi was a consummate singer, a true professional, who had mastered vocal artistry, blending his power-packed voice with warm, sultry tones to convey the emotions as the song demanded, and all of this delivered with finesse."
  },
