    "chunk_size_bytes": 1918,
    "chunk_text": "Forests \u2013 The Lifeline We Cannot Afford To Lose: Unfolding Crisis & Path To Restoration\n\nSri Lanka in 1881 was an emerald island. Its forests blanketed 84 percent of the land, with their canopies stretching endlessly across mountains and valleys. Today, a mere 29.7 percent remains. This is not merely statistics or limited to academic studies. Some commentators appear to be more worried about my doctorate rather than what I write. They need to understand that what we are discussing here is the story of a nation slowly severing its own lifeline.\n\nBetween 1960 and 2010, nearly one million hectares of forest vanished. Coffee and tea plantations marched across hillsides. Massive irrigation schemes consumed 25,000 acres in single projects. Post-war tourism zones claimed another 17,800 acres. Each cleared hectare represented not just lost trees, but fractured wildlife corridors, disrupted water cycles, and communities pushed toward inevitable collisions.\n\nIn 2025, the numbers tell a harrowing tale. 397 elephants dead \u2013 shot, electrocuted, blown apart by illegal explosives. These deaths are not random tragedies. They are the predictable outcome of animals with nowhere left to go, forced from vanishing habitats into farmlands by starvation.\n\nThe jackal population has plummeted 60 percent in two decades. Without these natural predators, peacocks have exploded beyond the dry zone into wet regions, and upset the ecological balances cultivated over thousands of years. Meanwhile, invasive species have transformed forests into food deserts. Elephants and monkeys have been pushed toward human settlements not due to aggression, but their desperation.\n\nConservation agencies requested LKR 325 million rupees for 2025. Initially, they received nothing. The previous year brought LKR 8 million against a request for LKR 129 million. Electric fences deteriorate, becoming death traps rather than deterrents."
