    "md5": "c9ad6ef20d9797507fc9d6553fe3fc6b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1688,
    "chunk_text": "Beyond bank discretion: Why Sri Lanka needs  independent mechanism to protect calamity-affected SMEs\n\nSri Lanka stands at a familiar crossroads. We can choose the old path, repair quickly, announce success, and wait for the next failure. Or we can choose a harder but wiser path, learn from our mistakes, enforce standards, and demand accountability\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s economic recovery is now threatened not only by past crises, but by a growing governance vacuum in how financial distress is resolved. At present, the fate of thousands of small and medium enterprises (SMEs), many affected by terrorism, pandemics, economic collapse, and natural disasters, rests almost entirely with individual commercial banks. This concentration of power, exercised through unilateral parate action, carries serious risks for fairness, transparency, and long-term economic stability.\n\nIn no mature financial system should the determination of business viability, recovery options, and asset disposal be left solely to creditor institutions with direct financial and commercial interests. Yet in Sri Lanka, there is no effective independent oversight body empowered to assess whether enforcement action is proportionate, justified, or in the broader national interest.\n\nSMEs in tourism, agriculture-linked services, manufacturing, and regional enterprises did not fail in isolation. Their distress is the result of nationally recognised shocks\u2014the Easter Sunday attacks, COVID-19 shutdowns, currency collapse, extreme interest rate volatility, and repeated climate-related disruptions. These are systemic events. Systemic problems require systemic solutions, not fragmented decisions taken bank by bank."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-01-07-dailyftlk-27f0fe6f",
    "num": "dailyftlk-27f0fe6f",
