    "md5": "8b00debd71754d5edab86a1e49ee6c6c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1547,
    "chunk_text": "My faux pas speaking French\n\nThe most striking aspect about UN employees, particularly those of European background, was their fluency in multiple languages. My superior in the UN Iraq Programme was a Swiss national from near Zurich and from the German speaking area of Switzerland. In addition to his native language of German, he could converse fluently in English, French, Italian and Spanish.\n\nOn average, a European bureaucrat could speak a minimum of three languages. I met a Swede in 2018 who was fluent in six languages. The Scandinavian countries educational system makes it mandatory for students to speak at least three languages before they graduate Primary School, with English and the mother tongue being mandatory.\n\nThe British system under which we were educated in the 1950s and 1960s had many drawbacks. One was the inadequacy of developing linguistic skills. Even English, which was an international language, was downgraded in schools after 1956. By right, Sri Lankans should be fluent is all three languages spoken in the island, with English providing anyone access to the world. Sinhala and Tamil both should have been taught to us. And we would have been tri-lingual and as a Sinhalese , I would have been fluent in the oldest language of the world \u2013 Tamil \u2013 by the time I was 10-years old.\n\nThe British, who did not pay much heed to linguistic skills, found to their horror the need to be proficient in other languages after the \u201cJewel in their Crown\u201d, India, won independence in 1947 after a hard-fought struggle."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-01-10-islandlk-abf7af08",
