    "md5": "2bc36e28121d6bdf77b4bf0d0ad76d0f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1770,
    "chunk_text": "The British, who did not pay much heed to linguistic skills, found to their horror the need to be proficient in other languages after the \u201cJewel in their Crown\u201d, India, won independence in 1947 after a hard-fought struggle.\n\nI was tested in my skills in spoken French again in late 2003. During the summer, the World Athletics Championship took place at Stade de France in St Denis. After the event, the games village apartments were up for rent. I secured one of them for a short period.\n\nThe roads of the village were named after famous athletes. The following incident took place whilst going in search of an apartment to rent: I met a passer-by after alighting from the Metro at St Dennis and, in my stuttering French, inquired;,\u201dExcusez-moi, Monsieur, o\u00f9 est la rue Jesse Owens s\u2019il vous pla\u00eet. Et pouvez-vous me donner des indications?\u201d which roughly translates to: \u201cExcuse me please Sir, where is Jesse Owens Street please? And can you direct me?\u201d\n\nThe man looked non plussed and after repeating my question, he said: \u201cS\u2019il vous pla\u00eet \u00e9crivez-le pour moi\u201d, meaning, \u201cPlease write down for me.\u201d So, I wrote down in French. And he laughed. \u201cCe n\u2019est pas rue Jesse Owens, c\u2019est rue Jessowens\u201d \u201cIt is not Jesse Owens, it is pronounced Jessowens\u201d, he explained in English.\n\n\u201cWhen a word ends or begin with a vowel, it is pronounced in French by joining together both adjacent words together and pronounced as a single word\u201d. Thus \u201cJesse Owens\u201d is \u201cJessowens\u201d. So, when one says, \u201cJe Vous Aime Beacoup\u201d (I love you very much) it is pronounced as one word. \u201cJevaisaimebeacoup\u201d. A famous Nat King Cole song of the \u201850s, the first line of which is \u201cDarling Jevaisembeacoup, means I love you, honestly I do\u201d"
  },
