    "md5": "3837e1c833a397bde0a2bd9a3c494f20",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1899,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cWhen a word ends or begin with a vowel, it is pronounced in French by joining together both adjacent words together and pronounced as a single word\u201d. Thus \u201cJesse Owens\u201d is \u201cJessowens\u201d. So, when one says, \u201cJe Vous Aime Beacoup\u201d (I love you very much) it is pronounced as one word. \u201cJevaisaimebeacoup\u201d. A famous Nat King Cole song of the \u201850s, the first line of which is \u201cDarling Jevaisembeacoup, means I love you, honestly I do\u201d\n\nHowever, in sharp contrast, there was a female Kurdish employee who was an exemplary worker with commendable output. Her strengths were in planning and financial management. Towards the end of my tenure, I praised her, in an email, for doing \u201cYeoman Service\u201d. However, she was agitated with this praise. It had the opposite effect on her. Fortunately for me, the director of the division, who was a Moroccan, understood what had happened and came to my rescue. In Arabic, which the Kurdish employee was familiar with, yeoman meant the \u201cDonkey\u2019s Keeper\u201d.\n\nThe Director, with the aid of an \u201cEnglish to French Dictionary\u201d was able to convince the employee that I really meant a compliment. Fortunately, in the French dictionary \u201cYeoman Service\u201d was defined as \u201cExlempaire Traville\u201d. The latter meant \u2018exemplary work\u201d, as it was in English.\n\nPhew! An impasse and a tricky situation were sorted. It was my \u201cFaux Pas\u201d and a good lesson learned in the need for checking translations to prevent cultural misunderstandings. Looking back on this incident, I realized where the original misunderstanding was. Even the original English meaning, according to the etymology of the word, was \u201can attendant in a noble household\u201d. In Arabic the word may have taken a pejorative meaning, that of \u201ca donkey\u2019s keeper\u201d. It was only later, even in English, that yeomen\u2019s service came to mean, \u201cgood, efficient service\u201d."
  },
