    "chunk_size_bytes": 1775,
    "chunk_text": "Brief proposal for re-settling \u2018Ditwah\u2019- triggered landslide victims\n\nThe captioned Cyclone has caused severe damage from floods and landslides in Sri Lanka. In the past too some flooding and landslides have occurred almost annually. The last SW monsoons were followed by a severe cyclone. The consequence was much greater flooding and we experienced a disproportionate increase in serious damage through landslides.\n\nThe official death toll from landslides alone as at early December was estimated at 618 with a great many people still missing, many of whom have to be presumed as being dead. With the onset of the NE Monsoon, still more landslides are predicted. As the title indicates, this present proposal is particularly focused on dealing with the consequences of these landslides.\n\nThe damage though of a very much greater magnitude this year, the locations are as before mainly confined to the Wet Zone hill slopes. Much of the past practice has generally been to attempt rebuilding such damaged houses and resettling affected people in similar nearby terrain. Although this had achieved some limited success in the past, in today\u2019s context of extreme and very extensive landslide damage, it is really unthinkable to even attempt to do so.\n\nThe concern focused here is on those who were resident in the Wet Zone hill slopes and have since been severely affected by the recent landslides.\n\nThe proposal is to resettle them not where they were nor in near-by Wet Zone areas but in appropriate locations within the Dry Zone which are likely to be less congested. This will not only relieve Wet Zone rural congestion in the hilly terrains but also help Dry Zone development effort which has been an increasingly important strategy for us over a great many decades."
  },
