    "md5": "c5d77cf89155b5e4c822b60f14896b54",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1815,
    "chunk_text": "Lending rates edge up amid private sector credit growth\n\nKey monetary indicators for the first week of January 2026 point to sustained credit expansion to the private sector alongside a slight tightening in lending rates, as the economy continues its recovery path.\n\nThe Weekly Average Weighted Prime Lending Rate (AWPR) rose by 12 basis points to 9.19% for the week ending 09 January 2026. This increase comes despite a stable policy corridor, with the Standing Deposit Facility Rate (SDFR) and Standing Lending Facility Rate (SLFR) holding at 7.25% and 8.25%, respectively.\n\nA standout figure is the robust growth in private sector credit. Outstanding credit to the private sector increased by Rs. 262.6 billion in November 2025 alone, recording a significant year-on-year growth of 26.0%. In contrast, Net Credit to the Government from the banking system decreased by Rs. 64.0 billion during the same month, indicating a shift in banking sector exposure.\n\nMarket liquidity remained in surplus, with total outstanding liquidity at Rs. 171.03 billion by January 9. The Average Weighted Call Money Rate (AWCMR) saw a slight decrease to 7.96%.\n\nThe positive sentiment extended to the Colombo Stock Exchange. The All Share Price Index (ASPI) gained 3.46% over the week to reach 23,654.30 points, while the S&P SL 20 Index rose 5.12% to 6,503.02 points.\n\nAnalysts see the strong private credit growth as a sign of rebounding business investment and consumption, but say the uptick in lending rates need to be monitored for its impact on borrowing costs.\n\nNTB upgraded to \u2018Buy\u2019 on strong earnings and strategic acquisition\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
